<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rhön</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rhön Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rhön is a range of low volcanic mountains in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> meet. Its highest peak is the Wasserkuppe, at 950 metres, known as the birthplace of gliding. Much of it belonged to the prince-abbots of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a>, and it is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> biosphere reserve.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhon.jpg" alt="Rhön" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jörg Braukmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rh%C3%B6n_von_Norden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Rhön Mountains are a group of low mountains in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, around the border area where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> meet. They lie at the extreme southeast end of the East Hesse Highlands and are partly the result of ancient volcanic activity. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> and its valley separate them from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vogelsberg">Vogelsberg</a> to the west. The range is bounded by the Knüll to the northwest, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a> to the northeast, the Grabfeld to the southeast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-franconia">Lower Franconia</a> to the south and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spessart">Spessart</a> to the southwest.</p><p>Geographers divide the Rhön by the effects of volcanic activity into the Anterior Rhön, the Kuppen Rhön and the High Rhön. The High Rhön covers 344 square kilometres, and its plateaux, starting at 600 to 700 metres, are covered by solid basalt. Its highest peak is the Wasserkuppe, about 950 metres, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. The kuppen of the surrounding Kuppen Rhön are the stumps of weathered former volcanoes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> rises on the Wasserkuppe and is the left headstream of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>, and the Franconian Saale, the Sinn and the Ulster also rise in or near the range.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name Rhön is often thought to derive from a Celtic word, raino, meaning hilly, though other explanations are possible. A Celtic presence is well established, with an important Celtic town at the Milseburg. Up to the 10th century parts of the Rhön belonged to the Altgau Buchonia, a term coined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in Late Antiquity for an ancient beech forest. Monks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda-abbey">Fulda Abbey</a> used the name for the area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> and more distant parts of the Rhön. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> beech was an important raw material, and large-scale clearing produced the land of open spaces, of which about 30 per cent is forested today.</p><p>The far view from the heights made them sites for hilltop castles. Hauneck Castle on the Stoppelsberg oversaw the ancient road Antsanvia and protected the villages of the Haune valley, and its ruins can still be seen. The Würzburg Defences, a landwehr, were built on the High Rhön to protect its farmers. A Christian community, the Bruderhof, lived in the Rhön from 1926 until Nazi persecution dissolved it in 1937.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1991 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared parts of the Rhön a Biosphere Reserve on account of its high-altitude ecosystem. The area is rich in habitats and species, among them raised bogs such as the Red Moor and the Black Moor and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> thistle, the symbol of the region. The mountains attract hikers, with nearly 6,000 km of trails, and gliding enthusiasts have come since the early 20th century. Observation Post Alpha, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> lookout, is now a memorial.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rhön Mountains” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The prince-abbots ruled a sizeable territory in the <b>Rhön</b> and the Fulda valley, with the support of a powerful noble chapter.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-abbey-of-fulda">Prince-Abbey of Fulda</a></li></ul></section>
